Q('p1','Field of an infinite charged sheet','For an ideal infinite sheet with surface charge density σ, the field magnitude on either side is:','|σ|/(2ε₀)',['|σ|/ε₀','|σ|/(4πε₀r²)','Zero everywhere'],'A pillbox has two contributing faces: 2EA = |σ|A/ε₀.')
Q('p1','Field inside and outside a charged shell','At a point strictly inside a uniformly charged thin spherical shell, the electric field is:','Zero',['kQ/r²','kQ/R²','kQr/R³'],'Spherical symmetry and zero enclosed charge give E = 0 inside the shell.')
Q('p1','Axial field of a dipole','Far from a short electric dipole on its axis, doubling the distance changes the field magnitude by a factor of:','1/8',['1/2','1/4','8'],'The dipole far field varies as 1/r³.')
Q('p1','Halving the equatorial distance','A far-away point on a dipole’s equatorial line moves to half its distance. The field magnitude becomes:','8 times',['2 times','4 times','Half'],'The short-dipole equatorial field varies as 1/r³, so (r/(r/2))³ = 8.')
Q('p2','Find the electric field from V(x)','If V(x) = 10 − 50x volts, with x in metres, what is Eₓ?','+50 N/C',['−50 N/C','+10 N/C','Zero'],'Eₓ = −dV/dx = +50 N/C.')
Q('p2','Work on the dipole’s equatorial plane','A charge is moved between two points on a dipole’s equatorial plane. The electrostatic work is:','Zero',['Always positive','Always negative','Dependent only on the distance travelled'],'The dipole potential is zero at both points, so qΔV = 0.')
Q('p2','Remove a dielectric after disconnecting the battery','A dielectric of constant K is removed from a charged capacitor after disconnecting its battery. Its stored energy becomes:','K times the original',['1/K times the original','K² times the original','Unchanged'],'Charge stays fixed. C becomes C/K, so U = Q²/(2C) becomes KU.')
Q('p2','Energy in ten parallel capacitors','Ten 1 μF capacitors in parallel are charged to 100 V. Their total energy is:','0.05 J',['0.005 J','0.5 J','5 J'],'C = 10 μF; U = ½CV² = ½ × 10⁻⁵ × 10⁴ = 0.05 J.')
Q('p3','A cell drops from 8 V to 4 V','A cell has EMF 8 V and terminal voltage 4 V while delivering 4 A. Its internal resistance is:','1 Ω',['2 Ω','0.5 Ω','4 Ω'],'r = (E − V)/I = (8 − 4)/4 = 1 Ω.')
Q('p3','Find both E and r','A cell gives 5 V at 2 A and 4 V at 4 A. Find its EMF and internal resistance.','6 V and 0.5 Ω',['5 V and 0.5 Ω','6 V and 2 Ω','4 V and 1 Ω'],'5 = E − 2r and 4 = E − 4r. Subtract: r = 0.5 Ω, then E = 6 V.')
Q('p3','Derive I = neAvd','At fixed n and cross-sectional area A, doubling the drift speed changes the current to:','Twice its original value',['Half its original value','Four times its original value','The same value'],'I = neAv_d, so current is directly proportional to drift speed.')
Q('p3','Which series wire heats more?','Equal-sized copper and nichrome wires carry the same current in series. Which dissipates more power?','Nichrome, because its resistance is greater',['Copper, because its conductivity is greater','Both equally, because current is equal','The wire with lower resistance'],'For the same I, P = I²R. Equal geometry means the larger resistivity gives larger R.')
Q('p4','Loop field on its axis','The central field of an N-turn circular coil of radius R carrying current I is:','μ₀NI/(2R)',['μ₀NI/(2πR)','μ₀NI/R²','μ₀I/(2NR)'],'The single-loop result is μ₀I/(2R); N coincident turns multiply it by N.')
Q('p4','Convert a galvanometer to an ammeter','To convert a galvanometer of resistance G and full-scale current I_g into an ammeter of range I, use:','A parallel shunt I_gG/(I − I_g)',['A series resistance I_gG/(I − I_g)','A parallel shunt IG/I_g','A series resistance G'],'Parallel branches have equal voltage: I_gG = (I − I_g)S.')
Q('p4','Torque on a rectangular current loop','A galvanometer uses a radial magnetic field mainly to make:','Torque proportional to current throughout its working range',['Torque independent of current','The coil’s resistance zero','The magnetic flux constant at zero'],'The radial field keeps the loop normal perpendicular to B, so τ = NIAB.')
Q('p4','Select particles of one speed','Charged particles pass undeflected through crossed fields E and B. Their speed is:','E/B',['B/E','EB','E²/B'],'For opposite electric and magnetic forces, qE = qvB.')
Q('p5','Diamagnetic versus paramagnetic response','Which susceptibility–response pair describes a diamagnet?','χ < 0; weakly repelled',['χ > 0; weakly repelled','χ < 0; strongly attracted','χ = 0; permanently magnetised'],'Its induced magnetisation opposes the applied field, giving negative susceptibility.')
Q('p5','Bring a diamagnet near either pole','A diamagnetic sample is brought near each pole of a magnet separately. It is:','Repelled by either pole',['Attracted by either pole','Attracted by north, repelled by south','Repelled by north, attracted by south'],'The induced moment reverses when the field reverses; the sample still prefers weaker field.')
Q('p5','Three classes of magnetic materials','Which set correctly matches material and behaviour?','Bismuth: diamagnetic; aluminium: paramagnetic; iron: ferromagnetic',['Bismuth: ferromagnetic; aluminium: diamagnetic; iron: paramagnetic','Bismuth: paramagnetic; aluminium: ferromagnetic; iron: diamagnetic','All three are ferromagnetic'],'These are standard examples of the three magnetic classes.')
Q('p6','Self-inductance of a long solenoid','For a long air-core solenoid, doubling N while keeping A and length fixed makes L:','4 times as large',['2 times as large','Half as large','Unchanged'],'L = μ₀N²A/l, so L depends on the square of the turn count.')
Q('p6','Why a closed coil resists an approaching magnet','The resistance felt while pushing a magnet into a closed coil follows:','Lenz’s law and conservation of energy',['Conservation of charge alone','A permanent increase in magnetic flux without current','Electrostatic shielding'],'The induced current opposes the flux change; mechanical work supplies electrical energy.')
Q('p6','Rotating rod EMF','A 0.50 m rod rotates about one end at 60 rpm in a perpendicular 4.0 mT field. Its EMF is approximately:','3.14 mV',['31.4 mV','0.314 mV','6.28 V'],'ω = 2π rad/s; ε = ½BωL² = 0.00314 V.')
Q('p7','Ideal step-up transformer','An ideal transformer has N_s/N_p = 5 and receives 5 kW at 200 V. What are I_p and V_s?','25 A and 1000 V',['5 A and 1000 V','25 A and 40 V','1000 A and 25 V'],'I_p = 5000/200 = 25 A; V_s = 5 × 200 = 1000 V.')
Q('p7','Series LCR resonance','At series LCR resonance, impedance and power factor are respectively:','R and 1',['Zero and 0','R and 0','X_L + X_C and 1'],'X_L = X_C cancels the net reactance; Z = R and voltage is in phase with current.')
Q('p7','Power in an ideal inductor','Average power over a full AC cycle in an ideal inductor is:','Zero',['V_rms I_rms','Half of V_rms I_rms','Always negative'],'The phase difference is π/2, so average power V_rms I_rms cosφ = 0.')
Q('p8','Current in the gap of a charging capacitor','For a vacuum capacitor gap, displacement current is:','ε₀ dΦ_E/dt',['ε₀Φ_E','dΦ_B/dt','Zero whenever no electrons cross the gap'],'A changing electric flux produces displacement current even without charge crossing the gap.')
Q('p8','Identify the radiation','Which pair matches aircraft radar and an electron-bombarded metal target?','Microwaves and X-rays',['Infrared and radio waves','Gamma rays and microwaves','Visible light and infrared'],'Radar commonly uses microwaves; decelerating high-speed electrons in a target produce X-rays.')
Q('p8','Wave entering a dielectric','An EM wave enters a medium with ε_r = 3/2 and μ_r = 8/3. Its speed and frequency become:','c/2 and unchanged frequency',['2c and unchanged frequency','c/2 and half the frequency','c and half the frequency'],'v = c/√(ε_rμ_r) = c/2. The source fixes the frequency across the boundary.')
Q('p9','Concave mirror image','An object lies 30 cm in front of a concave mirror of radius 40 cm. Under Cartesian signs, its image distance is:','−60 cm',['+60 cm','−12 cm','+12 cm'],'f = −20 cm, u = −30 cm. 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1/60.')
Q('p9','Design a symmetric convex lens','A symmetric biconvex lens has n = 1.55 and f = 20 cm in air. Each radius has magnitude:','22 cm',['11 cm','44 cm','36.4 cm'],'Lens maker gives R = 2(n − 1)f = 2 × 0.55 × 20 = 22 cm.')
Q('p9','Why light stays inside an optical fibre','For total internal reflection at a fibre’s core–cladding boundary, the core should have:','Higher refractive index, with incidence above the critical angle',['Lower refractive index, with incidence above the critical angle','Higher refractive index, with incidence below the critical angle','The same refractive index as the cladding'],'TIR requires travel from denser to rarer medium and incidence greater than the critical angle.')
Q('p9','Astronomical telescope in normal adjustment','A refracting telescope in normal adjustment has tube length:','f_o + f_e',['f_o − f_e','f_o/f_e','2f_e'],'The objective’s focal plane coincides with the eyepiece’s first focal plane.')
Q('p10','Calculate fringe spacing','For λ = 600 nm, D = 1.20 m and d = 0.1 mm, YDSE fringe width is:','7.2 mm',['0.72 mm','72 mm','0.072 mm'],'β = λD/d = (6×10⁻⁷ × 1.2)/(10⁻⁴) m = 7.2 mm.')
Q('p10','Unequal intensities interfere','Coherent beams of intensities I and 4I interfere. I_max:I_min is:','9:1',['4:1','3:1','Infinity'],'Amplitudes have ratio 1:2, so the extrema are (1+2)²I and (2−1)²I.')
Q('p10','Change the geometry of YDSE','In YDSE, doubling slit separation while holding λ and D fixed makes fringe width:','Half',['Double','Four times','Unchanged'],'β = λD/d; fringe width is inversely proportional to slit separation.')
Q('p10','Huygens construction and refraction','A wavefront is a surface whose points have the same:','Phase',['Amplitude necessarily','Speed in every medium','Distance from every source'],'A wavefront joins points of equal phase; Huygens wavelets construct its later position.')
Q('p11','Find work function from stopping potential','Light of wavelength 200 nm gives stopping potential 0.80 V. Using hc = 1240 eV nm, the work function is:','5.40 eV',['6.20 eV','7.00 eV','0.80 eV'],'Photon energy = 1240/200 = 6.2 eV; subtract K_max = 0.8 eV.')
Q('p11','Compare maximum photoelectron speeds','Photon energies 3.3 eV and 11.3 eV illuminate a metal with work function 2.3 eV. The first-to-second maximum speed ratio is:','1:3',['1:9','3:1','9:1'],'The kinetic energies are 1 and 9 eV; speeds scale as √K.')
Q('p11','Interpret the stopping-potential slope','A V_s versus frequency graph has slope m. Electron charge magnitude equals:','h/m',['hm','m/h','h²/m'],'V_s = (h/e)f − φ/e, giving m = h/e.')
Q('p11','Electron, proton and deuteron wavelengths','At equal nonrelativistic speeds, de Broglie wavelengths follow:','λ_e > λ_p > λ_d',['λ_d > λ_p > λ_e','λ_e = λ_p = λ_d','λ_p > λ_e > λ_d'],'λ = h/(mv). The electron is lightest and the deuteron heaviest.')
Q('p12','Energy in the third excited state','Hydrogen in its third excited state has total energy:','−0.85 eV',['−1.51 eV','−3.40 eV','+0.85 eV'],'Third excited means n = 4; E = −13.6/16 = −0.85 eV.')
Q('p12','Orbital period scaling','In the Bohr model, T_n varies with principal quantum number as:','n³',['n','n²','1/n³'],'T = 2πr/v; r ∝ n² and v ∝ 1/n.')
Q('p12','Closest approach when speed halves','An alpha particle’s head-on closest approach is d. Halving its initial speed changes this to:','4d',['2d','d/2','d/4'],'At closest approach, kinetic energy becomes Coulomb potential energy; d ∝ 1/v².')
Q('p13','Binding energy of a deuteron','For a deuteron, Δm = 0.002389 u. With 1 u c² = 931.5 MeV, total binding energy is approximately:','2.225 MeV',['1.113 MeV','0.002389 MeV','2225 MeV'],'B = Δmc² = 0.002389 × 931.5 = 2.225 MeV. Divide by two only for energy per nucleon.')
Q('p13','Which nucleus is more tightly bound?','When light nuclei fuse and release energy, the product generally has:','Higher binding energy per nucleon',['Lower binding energy per nucleon','Greater total rest mass than all reactants','Zero mass defect'],'A more tightly bound product has lower total rest mass; the difference supplies released energy.')
Q('p13','Why nuclear density is nearly constant','From R = R₀A^(1/3), nuclear density is approximately:','Independent of A',['Proportional to A','Proportional to A²','Inversely proportional to A'],'Nuclear mass and volume both scale with A, so their ratio is roughly constant.')
Q('p14','Doping germanium with arsenic','Arsenic-doped germanium is:','n-type, with electrons as majority carriers',['p-type, with holes as majority carriers','Positively charged overall','An insulator without mobile carriers'],'Pentavalent arsenic donates an electron in a tetravalent host; the bulk remains neutral.')
Q('p14','Currents in an unbiased junction','At equilibrium in an unbiased p–n junction, drift and diffusion currents are:','Equal in magnitude and opposite in direction',['Both necessarily zero individually','Equal in magnitude and in the same direction','Unequal, producing a steady net current'],'The two carrier-transport processes balance, giving zero net current.')
Q('p14','Forward bias and the barrier','Forward bias of a p–n junction makes the depletion layer:','Narrower and the potential barrier lower',['Wider and the potential barrier higher','Narrower and the potential barrier higher','Unchanged in width and barrier'],'The external field opposes the built-in field, reducing the barrier and depletion width.')
Q('p14','Full-wave rectification','A full-wave rectifier supplied by 50 Hz AC has output ripple frequency:','100 Hz',['25 Hz','50 Hz','0 Hz'],'Both half-cycles contribute a pulse, so the ripple frequency doubles.')
